@@ -110,3 +110,11 @@ Risks seen before the harness:
 - full-gate: Left out of bin/harness check: system tests (bin/harness test-system, need Chrome and a Mermaid.js download), the Bazaar non-ASCII test (needs the en_US.ISO8859-1 locale), bundle audit (needs network), and the PostgreSQL/MySQL and Ruby 3.3/3.4 matrix. The upstream workflows tests.yml and linters.yml still run all of them.
 - ci: The uw-harness package is not published on npm (npx -y uw-harness returned 404 here), so the new harness.yml workflow will fail until it is published. The gates were checked with the local build of uw-harness (check passes) and run directly from a clean shell.
 - deploy-explicit: Without human answers, docs/deploy.md and docs/observability.md keep open TODO blocks, so these two checks stay partial.
+
+## 2026-11-02: wiki toolbar syntax moved to JavaScript (#43259)
+
+The wiki toolbar no longer renders the syntax of each button. Redmine::WikiToolbar renders the format name (data-wiki-toolbar-format-value) and the button names, grouped; the text formatters (CommonMark and Textile) only give those. The wiki-toolbar Stimulus controller defines what each button inserts, per format, in its FORMATS table. Normal buttons use wiki-toolbar#apply with a button param, precode languages use wiki-toolbar#insertPrecode with a lang param, and keyboard shortcuts use one generic keydown action. Only the strong, em and ins buttons carry a shortcut attribute, and the keyboard actions are the generic one plus the two Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+P preview toggles.
+
+What was learned: Stimulus key filters require every modifier to match exactly, so the generic shortcut handler must reject Shift and Alt, or Ctrl+Shift+B would apply bold. The helper memoizes the toolbar per view, so a test that renders two formats must use a fresh view for each one.
+
+What is left: there is no JavaScript unit test runner in this repository, so the browser behavior is covered by the system tests in test/system/wiki_toolbar_test.rb and test/system/keyboard_shortcuts_test.rb, which need Chrome. The precode language is now a bare Stimulus param, so Stimulus typecasts it: a language name that looks like a number (for example 010 or 1e3) would not come back unchanged. The old markup kept the language inside a longer prefix string, so this is a new edge case; no default language is affected.
